From: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] linuxkpi: implement eth_type_trans() and eth_hw_addr_set()

eth_type_trans() ignored the frame entirely and returned a constant
ETHERTYPE_8023, so skb->protocol was always wrong and the Ethernet header
was never pulled.

Any consumer that switches on skb->protocol therefore misbehaves
silently.  brcmfmac is a clear example: brcmf_fweh_process_skb() drops
anything whose protocol is not ETH_P_LINK_CTL as its very first test, so
*every* firmware event was discarded.  The SDIO layer read the event
frames off the chip correctly and then threw them away, which made
association impossible with no error anywhere.

Implement it as Linux does: record the mac header, classify the
destination into skb->pkt_type, pull ETH_HLEN so skb->data is the payload,
and return the real ethertype.

eth_hw_addr_set() was likewise a no-op, leaving dev->dev_addr zeroed, so
an interface created from a driver that uses it had no MAC address.  Copy
the address.

Signed-off-by: Andrey Bodrov <ap.bodrov@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/etherdevice.h b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/etherdevice.h
index b9a4951de..6f2a6ff18 100644
--- a/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/etherdevice.h
+++ b/sys/compat/linuxkpi/common/include/linux/etherdevice.h
@@ -143,14 +143,54 @@ device_get_mac_address(struct device *dev, char *dst)
 static inline uint16_t
 eth_type_trans(struct sk_buff *skb, struct net_device *dev)
 {
-	pr_debug("%s: TODO\n", __func__);
-	return (htons(ETHERTYPE_8023));
+	const struct ethhdr *eh;
+
+	/*
+	 * This used to return a fixed ETHERTYPE_8023 without looking at the
+	 * frame at all, which silently broke every consumer that switches on
+	 * skb->protocol.  brcmfmac is one: brcmf_fweh_process_skb() drops any
+	 * skb whose protocol is not ETH_P_LINK_CTL, so *every* firmware event
+	 * (association results included) was discarded before reaching the
+	 * event handler -- the SDIO side dutifully read the frames and then
+	 * threw them away.
+	 *
+	 * Do what Linux does: remember where the Ethernet header starts, pull
+	 * it off so skb->data is the payload, and return the real ethertype.
+	 * Callers that need the header back use skb_mac_header().
+	 */
+	skb->dev = dev;
+	skb_reset_mac_header(skb);
+
+	if (skb->len < ETH_HLEN)
+		return (htons(ETHERTYPE_8023));
+
+	eh = (const struct ethhdr *)skb->data;
+	skb_pull(skb, ETH_HLEN);
+
+	if (is_multicast_ether_addr(eh->h_dest)) {
+		if (is_broadcast_ether_addr(eh->h_dest))
+			skb->pkt_type = PACKET_BROADCAST;
+		else
+			skb->pkt_type = PACKET_MULTICAST;
+	} else if (dev != NULL &&
+	    !ether_addr_equal(eh->h_dest, dev->dev_addr)) {
+		skb->pkt_type = PACKET_OTHERHOST;
+	}
+	/*
+	 * A frame addressed to us keeps the default 0.  Note this enum has no
+	 * PACKET_HOST, so 0 aliases to PACKET_BROADCAST; nothing in tree reads
+	 * it for that distinction (brcmfmac only compares against
+	 * PACKET_MULTICAST, for a statistics counter).
+	 */
+
+	/* h_proto is already in network byte order. */
+	return (eh->h_proto);
 }
 
 static inline void
 eth_hw_addr_set(struct net_device *dev, const u8 *addr)
 {
-	pr_debug("%s: TODO (if we want to)\n", __func__);
+	memcpy(dev->dev_addr, addr, ETH_ALEN);
 }
 
 static inline int
